&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class="OutlineElement Ltr SCXW86415226 BCX0" style="-webkit-user-drag: none; -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent; margin: 0px; padding: 0px; user-select: text; clear: both; cursor: text; overflow: visible; position: relative; direction: ltr; font-family: &amp;quot;Segoe UI&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;Segoe UI Web&amp;quot;, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 12px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;If you mention the fundraiser or show the flyer attached at any time on Wednesday, Jan. 14, 20 percent of food sales from your meal at Red Robin will go to&amp;nbsp;benefit&amp;nbsp;the Bend Police Department&rsquo;s Polar Plunge team.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class="OutlineElement Ltr SCXW86415226 BCX0" style="-webkit-user-drag: none; -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent; margin: 0px; padding: 0px; user-select: text; clear: both; cursor: text; overflow: visible; position: relative; direction: ltr; font-family: &amp;quot;Segoe UI&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;Segoe UI Web&amp;quot;, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 12px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;In 2025, Bend Police raised&amp;nbsp;more than $5,200 for Special Olympics athletes, and&amp;nbsp;we&rsquo;re&amp;nbsp;hoping to significantly increase that amount this year.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class="OutlineElement Ltr SCXW86415226 BCX0" style="-webkit-user-drag: none; -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent; margin: 0px; padding: 0px; user-select: text; clear: both; cursor: text; overflow: visible; position: relative; direction: ltr; font-family: &amp;quot;Segoe UI&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;Segoe UI Web&amp;quot;, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 12px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;Polar Plunge,&amp;nbsp;an annual fundraiser to&amp;nbsp;benefit&amp;nbsp;Special Olympics Oregon, takes place at 11 a.m. on Saturday, Feb. 28 at Riverbend Park, 799 SW Columbia Street, in Bend.&amp;nbsp;Participants take turns jumping into the chilly Deschutes River. The Bend Police Department has long been a staunch supporter and advocate for Special Olympics athletes, and&amp;nbsp;this is just one way we work to raise money to help them achieve their goals.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class="OutlineElement Ltr SCXW86415226 BCX0" style="-webkit-user-drag: none; -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent; margin: 0px; padding: 0px; user-select: text; clear: both; cursor: text; overflow: visible; position: relative; direction: ltr; font-family: &amp;quot;Segoe UI&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;Segoe UI Web&amp;quot;, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 12px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;: Tip-A-Cop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:wordvisicarriagereturn_msfontservice,calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Where&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;: Red Robin, 625 SW Powerhouse Drive, Bend&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:wordvisicarriagereturn_msfontservice,calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;When&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;: All day, with Bend PD on hand 4 p.m. to 8 p.m.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:wordvisicarriagereturn_msfontservice,calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Why&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;: To&amp;nbsp;benefit&amp;nbsp;Special Olympics athletes in Central Oregon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:wordvisicarriagereturn_msfontservice,calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;How&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;: If you mention the fundraiser or present the flyer, 20% of food sales will go to support Bend PD&rsquo;s Polar Plunge team&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class="OutlineElement Ltr SCXW86415226 BCX0" style="-webkit-user-drag: none; -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent; margin: 0px; padding: 0px; user-select: text; clear: both; cursor: text; overflow: visible; position: relative; direction: ltr; font-family: &amp;quot;Segoe UI&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;Segoe UI Web&amp;quot;, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 12px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class="OutlineElement Ltr SCXW86415226 BCX0" style="-webkit-user-drag: none; -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent; margin: 0px; padding: 0px; user-select: text; clear: both; cursor: text; overflow: visible; position: relative; direction: ltr; font-family: &amp;quot;Segoe UI&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;Segoe UI Web&amp;quot;, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 12px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:calibri,calibri_embeddedfont,calibri_msfontservice,sans-serif; font-size:12pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;&lt;strong style="background-color:rgb(255, 255, 255); color:rgb(65, 64, 66); font-family:arial,sans-serif; font-size:18px"&gt;&lt;a href="https://health.bentoncountyor.gov/from-survival-to-service-chris-mcfarland-brings-his-personal-experience-to-the-benton-county-act-team/#espanol" style="box-sizing: border-box; color: rgb(35, 72, 123); background-color: transparent; transition: 300ms;"&gt;* Espa&ntilde;ol a continuaci&oacute;n *&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;div class="post__content" style="box-sizing: border-box; color: rgb(65, 64, 66); font-family: &amp;quot;Open Sans&amp;quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 18px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"&gt;
&lt;div class="wp-block-image" style="box-sizing: border-box;"&gt;&lt;img alt="man standing in hallway with Crater Lake paining in the background and welcome sign." class="wp-image-9517" src="https://health.bentoncountyor.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Chris-McFarland.jpg" style="aspect-ratio:4 / 3; border-style:none; box-sizing:border-box; height:auto; max-width:100%; object-fit:cover; vertical-align:bottom; width:385px" /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Chris McFarland stands outside his office at the Benton Health Center in Corvallis, Ore. McFarland works as a counselor, supporting individuals with serious mental illness through community-based care. (Photo courtesy of Benton County)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chris McFarland&rsquo;s path to public service was shaped by hardship,&amp;nbsp;resilience&amp;nbsp;and a deep understanding of what it means to live on the margins.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, McFarland is a certified alcohol and drug counselor I and qualified mental health professional with the Benton County Health Department,&amp;nbsp;Behavioral Health Division. He serves on the Assertive Community Treatment team,&amp;nbsp;providing intensive, community-based behavioral health and substance use disorder services to individuals with serious mental illness&mdash;many of whom are experiencing homelessness and repeated contact with crisis and emergency systems.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The work is demanding and deeply personal. For McFarland, it&amp;nbsp;hits close to home.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Clients allow ACT team members into the most vulnerable parts of their lives, McFarland said, sharing fears, losses and hopes that are often hidden from the rest of the world. He said one of the things he is most proud of is when clients tell him they finally feel heard, respected and treated like a person,&amp;nbsp;not a diagnosis or a case file. Hearing that, he said, is a reminder that showing up consistently and leading with empathy&amp;nbsp;are what&amp;nbsp;truly matters.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;McFarland joined the U.S. Army in 1986 as a&amp;nbsp;&ldquo;homeless&amp;nbsp;17-year-old&amp;nbsp;high school dropout&rdquo;, hoping to find direction and stability. With limited options, he served as a light infantry soldier with the 1st Battalion, 87th Infantry Regiment, 10th Mountain Division. He completed basic and advanced infantry training at Fort Benning, Georgia, and was stationed at Fort Drum, New York&amp;nbsp;which is&amp;nbsp;known for some of the Army&rsquo;s toughest training conditions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;McFarland said it left a lasting impact.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;The military taught me how far I can push myself, physically and mentally,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;It taught me discipline, how to focus, how to take care of the person next to you, and that &lsquo;no&rsquo; or &lsquo;I can&rsquo;t&rsquo;&amp;nbsp;isn&rsquo;t&amp;nbsp;an option&amp;nbsp;when something needs to get done.&rdquo;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After leaving the Army, McFarland&rsquo;s life took a difficult turn. He spent more than a decade cycling through homelessness, substance use,&amp;nbsp;incarceration&amp;nbsp;and violence,&amp;nbsp;ultimately serving&amp;nbsp;time in prison from 1997 to 2000. Those experiences, he said, now inform how he approaches his work on the ACT team.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;I&rsquo;ve lived the chaos that many of our clients are living,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;I know what&amp;nbsp;it&rsquo;s&amp;nbsp;like to be stigmatized, discarded and forgotten. Those experiences&amp;nbsp;didn&rsquo;t&amp;nbsp;break me&mdash;they shaped me.&rdquo;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;McFarland initially pursued a degree in psychology, later shifting to public health, believing he did not want to work as a counselor. That changed during an internship at a residential drug and alcohol treatment facility for adolescents.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;That experience changed everything,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;I realized I&amp;nbsp;didn&rsquo;t&amp;nbsp;just enjoy counseling. I was good at it. More importantly, I realized I could use my lived experience to support people who felt invisible.&rdquo;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the ACT team, McFarland works where clients are, be that&amp;nbsp;in shelters, on the street, in hospitals or navigating court and crisis systems. The model emphasizes relationship-based care, harm&amp;nbsp;reduction&amp;nbsp;and long-term engagement.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Meghan Carlson, a qualified mental health professional with the ACT team, said McFarland&rsquo;s lived experience and openness about his journey have had a lasting impact not only on clients, but on the team itself.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;Chris&rsquo;s willingness to share his lived experience with us as clinicians has really changed how many of us approach client care,&rdquo; Carlson said. &ldquo;He has helped reshape how our ACT team functions, and in my 12 years working with this team, I can honestly say it&rsquo;s been for the better.&rdquo;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That impact is reflected in how he works with&amp;nbsp;clients&amp;nbsp;day to day.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;Chris meets people exactly where they are,&rdquo; said Carlson. &ldquo;He listens without judgment, shows up consistently and never loses sight of the person behind the diagnosis.&rdquo;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;County Administrator Rachel McEneny, who has spent time in the field&amp;nbsp;observing&amp;nbsp;McFarland as he worked directly with clients, said his approach reflects the values Benton County strives to uphold.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;Spending the day riding along with Chris and his colleagues in the field was a powerful and important experience for me as county administrator,&rdquo; said McEneny. &ldquo;Their work takes them everywhere from the&amp;nbsp;correctional facility&amp;nbsp;to city parks, the&amp;nbsp;hospital&amp;nbsp;and the Corvallis Daytime Drop-In Center.&amp;nbsp;The experience underscored the difficulty and complexity of the roles Chris&amp;nbsp;has to&amp;nbsp;juggle. It requires unique skills, compassion&amp;nbsp;and understanding.&rdquo;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;McFarland said working for Benton County feels like a culmination of everything his life has taught him.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;After living so many years in survival mode, being able to serve my community and help people find stability and dignity is incredibly meaningful,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;This work feeds my soul.&amp;nbsp;I honestly believe this&amp;nbsp;is exactly where&amp;nbsp;I&rsquo;m&amp;nbsp;supposed to be.&rdquo;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Espa&ntilde;ol&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;De la supervivencia al servicio: Chris McFarland aporta su experiencia personal al equipo ACT del&amp;nbsp;Condado de Benton&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="wp-block-image" style="box-sizing: border-box;"&gt;&lt;img alt="male standing in hallway with Crater Lake paining in the background and welcome sign." class="wp-image-9517" src="https://health.bentoncountyor.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Chris-McFarland.jpg" style="aspect-ratio:4 / 3; border-style:none; box-sizing:border-box; height:auto; max-width:100%; object-fit:cover; vertical-align:bottom; width:347px" /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Chris McFarland posa frente a su oficina en el Centro de Salud de Benton en Corvallis, Oreg&oacute;n. McFarland trabaja como consejero,&amp;nbsp;dando apoyo a personas con enfermedades mentales graves a trav&eacute;s de servicios de atenci&oacute;n comunitaria. (Foto cortes&iacute;a del&amp;nbsp;Condado de Benton)&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;La trayectoria de Chris McFarland en el servicio p&uacute;blico ha estado marcada por las dificultades, la resiliencia y una profunda comprensi&oacute;n de lo que significa vivir al margen de la sociedad.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Actualmente, McFarland es&amp;nbsp;profesional de salud mental&amp;nbsp;y&amp;nbsp;consejero&amp;nbsp;certificado en alcoholismo y drogadicci&oacute;n de nivel I&amp;nbsp;en&amp;nbsp;la Divisi&oacute;n de Salud&amp;nbsp;Mental&amp;nbsp;del Departamento de Salud del&amp;nbsp;Condado de Benton. Forma parte del equipo de Tratamiento Asertivo&amp;nbsp;Comunitario&amp;nbsp;(ACT&amp;nbsp;por sus siglas en ingl&eacute;s), que brinda servicios intensivos de salud&amp;nbsp;mental&amp;nbsp;y tratamiento de trastornos por consumo de sustancias en la comunidad a personas con enfermedades mentales graves, muchas de las cuales se encuentran&amp;nbsp;sin hogar&amp;nbsp;y&amp;nbsp;tienen contacto frecuente con los sistemas de crisis y emergencia.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El trabajo es exigente y profundamente personal. Para McFarland,&amp;nbsp;definitivamente es&amp;nbsp;algo&amp;nbsp;personal.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Los clientes permiten que los miembros del equipo de ACT&amp;nbsp;conozcan&amp;nbsp;los aspectos m&aacute;s vulnerables de sus vidas, explic&oacute; McFarland, compartiendo miedos, p&eacute;rdidas y esperanzas que a menudo ocultan al resto del mundo. Dijo que una de las cosas que m&aacute;s le enorgullecen es cuando los clientes le dicen que por fin se sienten escuchados, respetados y tratados como personas, no como un diagn&oacute;stico o un expediente. Escuchar eso le recuerda que estar presente de forma constante y actuar con empat&iacute;a es lo que realmente importa&amp;nbsp;para&amp;nbsp;&eacute;l.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;McFarland se&amp;nbsp;enlist&oacute;&amp;nbsp;al Ej&eacute;rcito de los Estados Unidos en 1986,&amp;nbsp;cuando era&amp;nbsp;&ldquo;un joven de 17 a&ntilde;os sin hogar que hab&iacute;a abandonado la escuela&amp;nbsp;preparatoria&rdquo;, con la esperanza de encontrar rumbo y estabilidad. Con pocas opciones, sirvi&oacute; como soldado de infanter&iacute;a ligera en el&amp;nbsp;1er&amp;nbsp;Batall&oacute;n del&amp;nbsp;87&ordm;&amp;nbsp;Regimiento de Infanter&iacute;a, de la&amp;nbsp;10&ordf;&amp;nbsp;Divisi&oacute;n de Monta&ntilde;a. Complet&oacute; el&amp;nbsp;entrenamiento&amp;nbsp;b&aacute;sico y avanzado de infanter&iacute;a en Fort&amp;nbsp;Benning, Georgia y fue destinado a Fort Drum&amp;nbsp;en&amp;nbsp;Nueva York,&amp;nbsp;un lugar&amp;nbsp;conocido por tener algunas de las condiciones de entrenamiento m&aacute;s exigentes del Ej&eacute;rcito.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;McFarland dijo que esta experiencia le dej&oacute; una huella imborrable.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;El ej&eacute;rcito me ense&ntilde;&oacute; hasta d&oacute;nde puedo llegar, f&iacute;sica y mentalmente&rdquo;, dijo. &ldquo;Me ense&ntilde;&oacute; disciplina, a concentrarme, a cuidar de la persona que est&aacute; a mi lado y que &lsquo;no&rsquo; o &lsquo;no puedo&rsquo; no son opciones cuando hay que hacer algo&rdquo;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Despu&eacute;s de dejar el ej&eacute;rcito, la vida de McFarland&amp;nbsp;enfrent&oacute;&amp;nbsp;una&amp;nbsp;etapa&amp;nbsp;dif&iacute;cil&amp;nbsp;de&amp;nbsp;su&amp;nbsp;vida. Pas&oacute; m&aacute;s de una d&eacute;cada&amp;nbsp;por&amp;nbsp;per&iacute;odos de falta de vivienda, consumo de sustancias, encarcelamiento y violencia y finalmente cumpli&oacute; condena en prisi&oacute;n de 1997 a 2000. Esas experiencias ahora influyen en la&amp;nbsp;manera&amp;nbsp;en que&amp;nbsp;hace&amp;nbsp;su trabajo en el equipo ACT.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;He vivido el caos que viven muchos de nuestros clientes&rdquo;, dijo&amp;nbsp;McFarland.&amp;nbsp;&ldquo;S&eacute; lo que se siente&amp;nbsp;el ser estigmatizado, marginado y olvidado. Esas experiencias no me destruyeron, me moldearon&rdquo;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inicialmente, McFarland estudi&oacute; psicolog&iacute;a, pero luego se cambi&oacute; a salud p&uacute;blica, creyendo que no quer&iacute;a trabajar como consejero. Eso cambi&oacute;&amp;nbsp;cuando&amp;nbsp;trabaj&oacute;&amp;nbsp;como&amp;nbsp;pasante&amp;nbsp;estudiantil&amp;nbsp;en un centro residencial de tratamiento de drogas y alcohol para adolescentes.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;Esa experiencia lo cambi&oacute; todo&rdquo;, dijo.&ldquo;Me di cuenta de que no solo disfrutaba de la consejer&iacute;a, sino que era bueno en ello. Y lo que es m&aacute;s importante, me di cuenta de que pod&iacute;a usar mi experiencia para apoyar a personas que se sent&iacute;an invisibles&rdquo;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En el equipo ACT, McFarland trabaja donde se encuentran los clientes, ya sea en refugios, en la calle, en hospitales o navegando por los sistemas judiciales y de atenci&oacute;n de crisis. El modelo enfatiza la atenci&oacute;n basada en las relaciones, la reducci&oacute;n de&amp;nbsp;riesgos&amp;nbsp;y&amp;nbsp;el compromiso a largo plazo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Meghan Carlson, profesional de salud mental cualificada del equipo ACT, afirm&oacute; que la experiencia personal de McFarland y su franqueza al compartir su historia han tenido un impacto duradero no solo en los clientes, sino tambi&eacute;n en el propio equipo.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;La disposici&oacute;n de Chris a compartir su experiencia personal con nosotros, los profesionales cl&iacute;nicos, ha cambiado la forma en que muchos de nosotros&amp;nbsp;manejamos&amp;nbsp;la atenci&oacute;n al cliente&rdquo;, dijo Carlson. &ldquo;Ha contribuido a transformar el funcionamiento de nuestro equipo ACT y en mis 12 a&ntilde;os trabajando con este equipo, puedo decir con toda sinceridad que ha sido para&amp;nbsp;mejorar&rdquo;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ese impacto se refleja en la forma en que trabaja con los clientes a diario.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;Chris se relaciona con las personas tal como son&rdquo;, dijo Carlson. &ldquo;Escucha sin juzgar, est&aacute; presente de forma constante y nunca pierde de vista a la persona que hay detr&aacute;s del diagn&oacute;stico&rdquo;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;La&amp;nbsp;Administradora del&amp;nbsp;Condado, Rachel McEneny, quien ha pasado tiempo en el campo observando a McFarland mientras trabajaba directamente con los clientes, dijo que su enfoque refleja los valores que el&amp;nbsp;Condado de Benton se esfuerza por mantener. &ldquo;Pasar el d&iacute;a acompa&ntilde;ando a Chris y sus colegas en el campo fue una experiencia poderosa e importante para m&iacute; como&amp;nbsp;Administradora del&amp;nbsp;Condado&rdquo;, dijo McEneny. &ldquo;Su trabajo los lleva a todas partes, desde&amp;nbsp;la c&aacute;rcel&amp;nbsp;hasta los parques de la ciudad, el hospital y&amp;nbsp;el&amp;nbsp;Corvallis&amp;nbsp;Daytime&amp;nbsp;Drop-In Center. La experiencia&amp;nbsp;revel&oacute;&amp;nbsp;la dificultad y la complejidad de las funciones que Chris debe desempe&ntilde;ar.&amp;nbsp;Se necesitan&amp;nbsp;habilidades &uacute;nicas, compasi&oacute;n y comprensi&oacute;n&rdquo;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;McFarland dijo que trabajar para el&amp;nbsp;Condado de Benton se siente como la culminaci&oacute;n de todo lo que la vida le ha ense&ntilde;ado.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;Despu&eacute;s de vivir tantos a&ntilde;os en modo de supervivencia, poder servir a mi comunidad y ayudar a las personas a encontrar estabilidad y dignidad es incre&iacute;blemente significativo&rdquo;, dijo. &ldquo;Este trabajo alimenta mi alma. Sinceramente, creo que este es exactamente el lugar donde debo estar&rdquo;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:times new roman,times,serif"&gt;PORTLAND, Ore.&mdash;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:times new roman,times,serif"&gt;&lt;span style="color:black"&gt;Today, the Justice Department announced criminal charges against Luis Nino-Moncada, an illegal alien and known Tren de Aragua (TdA) associate living in the Portland, Oregon area for violently striking a federal law enforcement vehicle. During a targeted enforcement operation, Nino-Moncada is alleged to have repeatedly struck a federal law enforcement vehicle, placing Border Patrol agents in harm&rsquo;s way and resulting in significant damage to federal property. Nino-Moncada is in the country illegally, having previously been ordered removed by an immigration judge in 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:times new roman,times,serif"&gt;&lt;span style="color:black"&gt;&ldquo;Anyone who crosses the red line of assaulting law enforcement will be met with the full force of this Justice Department,&rdquo; said Attorney General Pamela Bondi. &ldquo;This man &mdash; an illegal alien with ties to a foreign terrorist organization &mdash; should NEVER have been in our country to begin with, and we will ensure he NEVER walks free in America again.&rdquo;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:times new roman,times,serif"&gt;&lt;span style="color:black"&gt;Nino-Moncada has been charged with aggravated assault of a federal officer with a deadly or dangerous weapon and depredation of federal property in excess of $1,000. Nino-Moncada is scheduled to make his Initial Appearance today at 1:30 PM PST.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:times new roman,times,serif"&gt;&lt;span style="color:black"&gt;According to the complaint, Border Patrol agents initiated a targeted immigration enforcement operation on a vehicle believed to belong to a female involved in a TdA prostitution ring. The female was sitting in the passenger-side seat of the vehicle at the time of the enforcement operation. This female had previously been arrested by Border Patrol in El Paso, Texas and later failed to report to immigration authorities. According to law enforcement reports, the female is believed to have been involved in a shooting stemming from a bad prostitution deal.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:times new roman,times,serif"&gt;&lt;span style="color:black"&gt;After commanding Nino-Moncada to exit the vehicle, Nino-Moncada is alleged to have placed his vehicle in reverse, colliding with an unoccupied Border Patrol vehicle with enough speed and force to cause significant damage. Nino-Moncada continued the forward and reverse maneuver multiple times, striking the Border Patrol vehicle multiple times. A Border Patrol agent interviewed by FBI special agents described being fearful that Nino-Moncada could strike them and other Border Patrol agents with the target vehicle. After the attack, Nino-Moncada and the female passenger fled the scene in the target vehicle.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:times new roman,times,serif"&gt;&lt;span style="color:black"&gt;Damage to the border patrol vehicle was significant. Serious damage was sustained to the front bumper, resulting in the bumper being torn from the vehicle, both headlights being destroyed, the front driver-side quarter panel being dented, including the fender liner being detached, and the driver side rear-passenger door and quarter panel being dented.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin-left:0in; margin-right:0in; text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family:times new roman,times,serif"&gt;&lt;span style="color:black"&gt;In an interview following the incident, Nino-Moncada admitted to intentionally ramming the Border Patrol vehicle in an attempt to flee and stated that he knew it was an immigration enforcement vehicle.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Terrebonne, OR &ndash;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; The Deschutes County Sheriff&rsquo;s Office is reminding residents about this week&rsquo;s community Town Hall meeting on Wednesday, January 14, from 5:30 p.m. to 7:30 p.m. at Terrebonne Community School.&lt;/p&gt;

